--TEST--
`Core\Sse->retry` puts the reconnection time on the wire as a block of its own, between events and dispatching none — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What a program says with it is "do not come back for an hour", ahead of a
// drain it can see coming. An event it had to attach that to would be an event
// the program did not otherwise want to send, so the wait is its own block: a
// `retry:` line with no `data:` line under it, which a client parses and
// dispatches nothing for.
var $stream = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->send("ready");
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::hours(1));
    $sse->send("draining");
});
echo $stream;
echo "--\n";

// It is neither the first thing on the stream nor the last, and it changes
// nothing about the events either side of it: the events a client dispatches
// are the ones that carried a payload, in the order they were written.
var $alone = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->retry(Core\Time\Duration::seconds(30));
});
echo $alone;
echo "--\n";
--EXPECT--
data: ready

retry: 3600000

data: draining

--
retry: 30000

--
